# Gate re-check: Python C2-S3, round 5, final base `be1f8d06`

Re-read the staged change on the rebased tree (6 files, +783/−42; index == working tree; nothing committed). All evidence from the staged files; no suites run.

## 1. `py/stop-as-return` and the delegate throw path — fix confirmed, fresh-eyes re-check clean

`py/delegate-step` (`prelude.cljc:445-459`) now routes only the **generator-delegate throw** outcome through `py/stop-as-return` (`462-468`); the send arm and the non-generator throw arm are untouched. I re-derived every throw case against CPython 3.9.6 (`gen_throw`/`gen_send_ex`/`_PyGen_FetchStopIterationValue` semantics):

| Case | Prelude (`file:line`) | CPython 3.9.6 | Match |
|---|---|---|---|
| thrown `StopIteration(7)`, delegate closed | closed passthrough `gen-switch:260-263` → `[:raise]` → consumed as `[:return 7]` | finished generator's throw raises as-is; delegator consumes `StopIteration` and resumes with `.value` | ✓ |
| thrown `MyStop(8, 9)` (subclass) | `py/subclass?` + `attrs["value"]` = 8 (init inherited via `py/class-lookup`) | same, `.value = args[0]` | ✓ |
| thrown `StopIteration` (class) | `throw-exc` instantiates → value None → completion None | same | ✓ |
| thrown into a **suspended** delegate, uncaught | delivered at its yield site; escapes → `py/gen-fail:297-304` → RuntimeError; arrives as `[:raise RuntimeError]` → **not** consumed | `gen_send_ex` converts identically → RuntimeError to caller | ✓ |
| thrown into a **sequence** delegate | `[:raise v]` raised in the outer (`458`); uncaught → PEP 479 at the outer's boundary | `AttributeError` on `.throw` cleared, original re-raised at the delegator's `yield from` → same | ✓ |
| delegate **body** raises StopIteration (send or throw) | body path hits `gen-fail` first, so only RuntimeError can arrive; the send arm (`457`) never passes through `stop-as-return` | PEP 479 RuntimeError | ✓ |
| outer's own handler raises StopIteration | `stop-as-return` only wraps the delegate's switch outcome; the outer's own raise is untouched → RuntimeError | same | ✓ |
| thrown StopIteration caught by the delegate (yield or return) | `[:yield]`/`[:return]` pass through unchanged (`465` false-branch) | same | ✓ |

Swallow-hunt: the only sources of `[:raise <StopIteration>]` from a generator delegate's switch are `gen-switch`'s closed/created passthroughs of the thrown message (`260-268`) — every body-raised one is already RuntimeError. Nothing else can be consumed. Confirmed no change to the round-1 conforming cases: send forwarding (`434`, `448-451`), close ordering (`445-447` + `py/gen-close`), already-executing (`258-259`), PEP 479 on the body, non-generator delegates. The fix's e2e test hand-verifies (`yield-from-thrown-stop-iteration-test`: "v 7"/"after", "v 8"/"after", "v None"/"after", then two `('generator raised StopIteration',)` — all CPython 3.9.6-correct) and fails without the fix (pre-fix cases 1-3 end in an uncaught RuntimeError).

**P3** | `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:467` | a StopIteration-subclass instance whose attrs lack `"value"` (a guest subclass overriding `__init__` without inheriting `StopIteration.__init__`) completes delegation with None; CPython's `_PyGen_FetchStopIterationValue` falls back to `args[0]` | None is a safe default and normally-defined subclasses are unaffected (init resolves through `py/class-lookup`); if closed later, fall back to `args`' first item when `"value"` is missing. No action requested this slice.

## 2. Sticky invalidation — fix confirmed

`py/iter-step` (`prelude.cljc:1775-1795`): before advancing, a `:keys-iter` source whose live count differs from its recorded size has that size rewritten to `-1` **on the iterator's own source map**, then the fall-through advance raises via `py/iter-at:1788-1793`. Count can never equal −1, so every later `next`/`for` step raises the RuntimeError forever — including after the dict is restored (the parity form does exactly this: `cell/set! dct before` then expects `true` again at `k`). Verified: each `iter()` call builds a fresh keys-iter map, so the poison cannot touch an unrelated iterator or the dict itself; legitimate exhaustion is unaffected (the `:src :py/None` path precedes the check); `next(it, default)` still raises (non-`:return` outcomes are never defaulted, `py/gen-result`); the pre-check and `iter-at`'s check are the same predicate on the same data with no guest code between, so the first error can never fire unpoisoned. The round-1 P3 (same-size delete+insert still silently continues; CPython's version tag) remains, disposition unchanged: out of scope.

## 3. The new tests — can fail, CPython-accurate, honest heap claim

- `yield-from-chain-with-changing-callers-test` (4-deep coverage: chain a→b→c, resumers = top level, guest function, plain generator, delegating generator; throw through three levels from a function): hand-computed `"c1" "c2" "b-list" "a-end" "done"`, log `[('c got', 'sent'), 'c fin', ('b got', 'c-ret'), ('a got', 'b-ret')]`, then `"c caught" "end"` — CPython 3.9.6-exact; exact-output on 4 evaluators, so any stale handler stack or misrouted message fails it.
- `yield-from-suspension-during-unwinding-test`: yields inside `finally`/`except` while KeyError/ValueError unwind through both levels; hand-computed `"1" "inner cleanup" "outer handler" "outer cleanup" "end"` and `"inner cleanup" "outer2 cleanup" "caller ('v',)"` — including the saved-exception-re-raised-after-resume semantics, CPython-exact.
- `reachable-heap-is-stable-under-delegation-test`: honest about what it measures. The docstring says it: the module has completed, the one suspended `h` is the only extra root, and the measure is the collector's output over that final state (`live-after-collection`, `e2e_c2_test.clj`), not a mid-run observation. Mechanically sound: `engine/collect` (`engine.cljc:2028-2040`) is the real stop-the-world collector; the root is a well-formed sealed `:cell-ref` rebuilt from the heap entry's own `{:value, :seal}` (`engine.cljc:1888`, ref shape `312-323`); `deep-size` follows continuation/closure payloads via `values/host-typed?` and counts cell refs as themselves (they are plain maps), so shared cells are counted once; alternating callers and 10-vs-1000 equality mean any per-yield or per-caller retention (stale continuations, flag-cell leaks, retained resumers) changes the number — it can fail. `^:slow` is on it and on `yield-from-long-range-test`; all seven master `^:slow` tags intact.

