`:require` in `e2e_c2_test.clj` carries both `yin.vm.engine` and `yin.vm.integer`, alphabetized, no duplicates, both used (`engine/collect` in the heap test; `integer/` at lines 879-880). No conflict markers in any of the six staged files. `git diff master -- float_address_test.cljc` shows exactly five changed lines — the five prelude-derived goldens (old `d99d4805…/d9185a98…/b3a62d04…/48586d14…/306f688f…` → new `5d0cf7ad…/47f4af6d…/6aef84ed…/b74fa7d9…/1024e8e0…`), re-minted once on this base; the hook-prelude golden and C3-S2's dict-keys golden are not among them. `py/key` (`prelude.cljc:1382`), `py/int-canon` (`1427`), `py/hash` (`1442`) are present from the base and absent from the staged diff. Each new prelude key is defined exactly once (`431`, `437`, `445`, `462`, `1758`, `1775`, `1797`); `"iter"` appears exactly twice (the builtin's name and the `builtin-names` entry).

## 5. Cross-host portability of the new code — clean

New prelude code uses only keyword equality, cell access, `assoc`/`get`, `data/count`, integer arithmetic, `py/conj`/`py/outcome` — no reader conditionals, no floats or unary minus, no regex/str helpers, no private mutable fields. The `(get src :py/type)` probe on a list-valued source (a cell) safely answers nil/false on every host before any `cell/get`. Both new parity forms are host-neutral: quoted vectors/maps (`'[outer …]`, `{:py/str "a"}`) are data read by `sexp->uast`, elements not host-evaluated; the `(list 'let '[…] (reduce (fn [acc s] (list 'py/conj acc s)) [] '[…]))` construction evaluates only at host time to build the form, and the reduce builds the conj chain in `a b c e f g h k` / `r1…v4` order matching the expected vectors, which I hand-traced to exact equality on the prelude semantics.

